.TH OB-LOGIN-SHELL 8 "September 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-login-shell \- login shell of SSO users on a host that records sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-login-shell
.RB [ \-l | \-\-login | \-i ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR COMMAND ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ob-login-shell
is the login shell that
.B libnss_openbastion
hands out to every user it resolves on a bastion or standalone host that
records sessions (the
.B force_shell
key of
.IR nss_openbastion.conf ,
written by
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8)
and
.BR ob-post-upgrade (8)).
It is not meant to be run by hand.
.PP
sshd never executes a
.B ForceCommand
itself: it runs it through the user's login shell, as
.IB "shell " "\-c '/usr/sbin/ob-session-recorder'" .
A shell reads startup files before it runs that command, and some belong to
the user: Debian's
.BR bash (1)
sources
.I ~/.bashrc
for
.B bash \-c
whenever
.B SSH_CLIENT
is set, and
.BR zsh (1)
reads
.I ~/.zshenv
for every invocation. With such a login shell, whatever those files hold runs
before the session recorder, and is not recorded.
.PP
.B ob-login-shell
reads no file the user controls and starts no shell. It has one way out: it
executes
.BR ob-session-recorder (8),
in the same process, with an environment it builds itself. The recorder then
starts the user's real shell inside
.BR script (1),
where reading
.I ~/.bashrc
is harmless: it is recorded.
.SH INVOCATIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " /usr/sbin/ob-session-recorder " \fR[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
sshd running the
.BR ForceCommand .
The recorder is executed with those options, and
.B SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND
(the command the client asked for) is passed on. The options must be plain
words (letters, digits and
.BR "_ . / = : , + \-" );
a
.B ForceCommand
with quoting or shell syntax is not recognised, and is handled as the next
case.
.TP
.BI \-c " COMMAND"
Anything else asking the login shell to run a command:
.BI "su \-c " COMMAND\fR,
.BI "sudo \-i \-u " "USER COMMAND" \fR,
an sshd with no
.B ForceCommand
for this user.
.I COMMAND
is
.B not
run. It becomes
.B SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND
and the recorder runs it, recorded.
.TP
no argument, \fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-login\fR, \fB\-i\fR
An interactive login: the console,
.BR "su \-" ,
.BR "sudo \-i" .
The recorder starts the real shell, recorded. An
.B SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND
inherited from the caller is not passed on.
.PP
Any other invocation (another option, arguments after
.BI \-c " COMMAND"\fR)
is refused and nothing runs. Every accepted path ends in the recorder: there
is no way through this program that is not recorded.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The recorder does not inherit the caller's environment. It gets:
.TP
.BR USER ", " LOGNAME ", " HOME
from the passwd entry of the real uid, not from the variables of the same
names;
.TP
.B SHELL
the shell the recorder starts for the session (see
.BR FILES );
.TP
.B PATH
.IR /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/games ;
.TP
.BR TERM ", " SSH_CLIENT ", " SSH_CONNECTION ", " SSH_TTY ", " SSH_AUTH_SOCK
when present and well-formed;
.TP
.BR LANG ", " LANGUAGE ", " LC_*
when they name a locale; a locale named by a path, which glibc would load from
that path, is dropped;
.TP
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ", " XDG_SESSION_ID ", " XDG_SESSION_TYPE ", " XDG_SESSION_CLASS
when well-formed (the runtime directory only as
.IR /run/user/UID );
.TP
.B SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND
as described under
.BR INVOCATIONS .
.PP
Everything else is dropped:
.BR BASH_ENV ,
.BR ENV ,
.BR SHELLOPTS ,
exported functions,
.BR LD_* ,
.BR OB_* ,
.BR LOCPATH ,
.BR GCONV_PATH ,
.BR TMPDIR ,
.BR TZ ,
and the rest.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/open-bastion/nss_openbastion.conf
.B default_shell
is the shell the recorder starts for the session (last occurrence wins,
surrounding quotes removed). The file is ignored unless it is a regular file
owned by root and writable by nobody else. Without it, or without the key,
.I /bin/bash
is used. A value that is not a plain absolute path to an executable file, or
that is
.B ob-login-shell
itself or the recorder (the recorder would start itself inside its own
recording, forever), is refused with a syslog warning and
.I /bin/bash
is used instead.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/ob-session-recorder
The only program this one executes.
.TP
.I /etc/shells
Lists
.I /usr/sbin/ob-login-shell
(the package and the setup add it), so that
.BR pam_shells (8)
and other checks of "a valid login shell" accept it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
On success it does not return: it becomes the recorder.
.TP
.B 1
The account could not be resolved, no usable shell exists for the session, or
the recorder could not be executed. The session is refused.
.TP
.B 2
Unsupported invocation. Nothing was run.
.SH NOTES
A backend has no
.B ForceCommand
and records nothing, and
.B ob-bastion-setup \-\-disable\-session\-recorder
installs no recorder: on those hosts
.B force_shell
is not set and SSO users keep an ordinary shell.
.PP
Local accounts in
.I /etc/passwd
are not resolved by
.BR libnss_openbastion ,
and keep the shell the administrator gave them. On a recording host, an
account that logs in over SSH goes through the same
.B ForceCommand
and has the same exposure; give it this shell with
.BR "chsh \-s /usr/sbin/ob-login-shell " \fIUSER\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ob-session-recorder (8),
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8),
.BR ob-post-upgrade (8),
.BR sshd_config (5),
.BR nsswitch.conf (5),
.BR shells (5)
.SH AUTHOR
